According to Newton's law of gravitation,

F=$\frac{G m_{1} m_{2}}{r^{2}}$where, $F=$ force between two objects of masses $m_{1}$ and $m_{2}$ and $r=$ distance between $m_{1}$ and $m_{2}$.

$\begin{array}{ll}
\therefore$& G=$\frac{F r^{2}}{m_{1} m_{2}}$\\
\Rightarrow & {[$\mathrm{G}]=\frac{[\mathrm{F}]\left[\mathrmr^{2}\right]}{\left[\mathrm{m}_{1}\right]\left[\mathrm{m}_{2}\right]}=\frac{\left[\mathrm{MLT}^{-2}\right] \left[\mathrmL^{2}\rright]}{[\mathrm{M}][\m$mathrm{M}]}= $\left[\mathrmM^{-1} \mathrm{~L}^{3} \mathrm{~T}^{-2}\right]$}
\end{array}

From Coulomb's law, force between two charges, F=$\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \frac{q_{1} q_{2}}{r^{2}} \begin{aligned}&\Rightarrow \quad \varepsilon_{0}=\frac{q_{1} q_{2}}{4 \pi r^{2} \bar{F}}\\
&\therefore \quad \text{ Unit$ of $} \quad \varepsilon_{0}=\frac{\mathrm{C} \cdot \mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{m}^{2} \mathrm{ N}}=\frac{\mathrm{C}^{2}}{\mathrm{ m}^{2} \mathrm{ N}}=\mathrm{C}^{2} \mathrm{ m}^{-2} \mathrm{ N}^{-1}
\end{aligned}$
